1 OCCU Salonniere
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://www.jewishencyclopedia.com/view.jsp?artid=504&letter=B
2 CONT
2 CONT BEER, AMALIE (née Amalie Wolf):
2 CONT
2 CONT German philanthropist and communal worker; died at Berlin June 22 (24), 1854. She was the wife of the banker Jacob Herz Beer, daugher of Liebmann Meyer Wolf (known as "the Berlin Crœsus"), and great-granddaughter of Lipmann Wolf T
2 CONC aussig. She was very charitable, and an active member of the Women's Aid Society for Wounded Soldiers, which was conducted under the patronage of Princess William of Prussia. In consideration of her valuable services, she received
2 CONC  from the king the Order of Queen Louise, being the first Jewess to be so distinguished. In order, furthermore, not to offend the pious Jewess, who was averse to wearing a cross, the king decreed that she should wear the ordinary 
2 CONC decoration of the first class in the form of a medallion attached to a ribbon. Her hospitable home in the Thiergartenstrasse, Berlin, was one of the most brilliant salons of the time, and was honored occasionally by the king's pre
2 CONC sence. Amalie Beer was the mother of the poet Michael Beer, of the composer Giacomo Meyerbeer, and of two other sons, Heinrich and Wilhelm.
2 CONT
2 CONT Bibliography:
2 CONT
2 CONT Kohut, Gesch. der Deutschen Juden, p. 775 (portrait, ib. p. 780);
2 CONT
2 CONT Allgemeine Deutsche Biographie, xxiv. 632 (s.v. Meyerbeer).
2 CONT
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Amalie_Beer
2 CONT
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.worldcat.org/title/miscellaneous-papers-1811-1914/oclc/58534208?ht=edition&referer=di
2 CONT
2 CONT April 20, 1811, copy of letter (in German) from Amalia Beer, mother of composer Giacomo Meyerbeer, to Professor Wolfsohn in Darmstadt. Mentions dealings with both her son and the composer [Carl Maria von] Weber. The original of th
2 CONC is letter is at Davidson College, N.C.
2 CONT
2 CONT August 5, 1914, broadside (in German) with patriotic essay, "An England," by Professor Otto von Gierke, criticizing England as being traitorous.
2 CONT
2 CONT October 16, 1914, copy of the Norddeutsche Allgemeine Zeitung, with article on the origins of the war and war news.
