1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Highest elder of all Jews in the Prussian states. General privileged from 1786. (General protection privilege from December 26th, 1786
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul Moßner writes the following about the duties of an elder of the Jewish community in Berlin:
2 CONT
2 CONT "The position of the elders of the Jewish community in Berlin was semi-official. According to the rescript of July 16, 1698, which ordered a new election of the elders, the elected should be presented" for the most gracious confir
2 CONC mation "(by the electoral prince)". According to the edict of January 24th, 1700, "the elders and directors should always stand and be liable for it (for the payment of protection money)". The Regiment for the Berlin Jews of Decem
2 CONC ber 7th, 1700 further stipulated that the elders, who were to be re-elected every three years, had to pay attention to the implementation of this regiment. State Archives Berlin-Dahlem, Rep. 21, No. 205, 1714 - 28, contains specia
2 CONC l regulations for the Jewish elders dated March 16, 1722. The Jewish communities represented autonomous bodies that had their own community assets, their own poor system, their own educational institutes, their own legal and tax s
2 CONC ystem, their own culture, their own jurisdiction, their own administration, their own houses of worship and cemeteries. It was headed by a number of elders (Parnassim), usually five who were elected by the congregation every three
2 CONC  years, and three self-elected community leaders. They represented the community vis-à-vis the state, they determined the budget, they executed the bonds, in their hands was the independent assessment of each community member for 
2 CONC tax, they transferred the money collected by the taxpayers to the state treasury, and they carried out a kind of supervision of trade , Change and moral attitude of the parishioners, they exercised a certain police jurisdiction, w
2 CONC hich they carried out with the help of the ban and the fines.The religious life led the rabbis. He was also entitled to jurisdictional violence in matters of money and debt, in matters of rites and ceremonies and similar matters. 
2 CONC He administered the judge's office alone or with the help of two assessors. He could expel the accused from the community, dictate fines and banish him (after Selma Stern, the Prussian State and the Jews, I, 102). "
2 CONT
2 CONT Paul Moßner also wrote the following about Moses Zwi:
2 CONT
2 CONT "Moses Abraham from Schwersens" is our (the Moßner family or von Moßner) ancestor on his tombstone in the cemetery in Berlin, Gr. Hamburger Straße 26, called. This does not mean that our ancestor was born in Schwersens, although t
2 CONC his is possible, but only that he was there before he came to Berlin. Schwersens was founded by contract dated 03.06.1621 between the landlords Sigismund von Grudno-Grudzinski and the community elder of Poznan as a branch municipa
2 CONC lity of Poznan. The first newcomers came from there. But it is also possible that the family comes from Vienna. After the expulsion of 1670, some of the displaced turned to the Greater Polish communities in Krotoschin, Lissa and P
2 CONC osen (M.Stern). Perhaps there is something else to be ascertained from the parish files: "The synagogue parish is said to have its oldest original documents in Poznan, two pieces of file with basic extracts, processual documents, 
2 CONC tax records, a compilation of the gifts from 1556 - 1825, two log books and an accounting book from the 18th century". (A. Warschauer, the Municipal Archives of the Province of Poznan, booklet of the messages of the Prussian archi
2 CONC ve administration. 1901. A. Warschauer, the emergence of a Jewish community, magazine for the history of the Jews in Germany, published by L. Geiger, Volume IV, 1890, p. 170). Go. pr.St.Archiv Rep 104 IV a No. 48 1737 - 50 "
