2 NOTE Automated translation of above document (unedited):
3 CONT
3 CONT "David Neumark, who exercised a decisive influence as the leader of the community (cf. Kaufmann, Expulsion, p. 27 and p. 59), was known to have been given the name Frank 1 by the authorities, a name which some of his descendants s
3 CONC ubsequently adopted and which has become the only common family name (for example: David Fränkl, the well-known teacher of Moses Mendelsohn). As already mentioned above in No. 331, he owes the name Fränkl to his father-in-law Jako
3 CONC b Koppel ha-Levi Heller-Wallerstein (above No. 52 and further below No. 581), who bore the name Fränkl from birth; cf. "Letters", p. 19, n. G, the reference to Müller in the journal of the Historical Association for Swabia. The na
3 CONC me Mirls, by which Jakob David Neumark is also known and which received a special brilliance through the importance of his children, is most likely to be traced back to his mother-in-law Mirl, the daughter of Meshulam Salman Thcom
3 CONC im (above no. 240), cf. “Letters”, 1. c. This circumstance explains why the two names Fränkl and Mirls are used by families of Levitical as well as non-Levitical origin.1
3 CONT Lands hu th (Toledoth Ansche ha-Schem, pp. 35-36) has provided information on David Neumark’s ancestry from a handwritten family tree dating from the 18th century; according to this, the ancestral home of the Wallerstein family wo
3 CONC uld have been. The first known ancestor Ahron Heller Wallerstein had a son HaGaon Koppel. His son HaGaon Naphtali Hirsch, rabbi in Fulda, was the father of our Jakob David Neumark, who is also mentioned with the title HaGaon. Rega
3 CONC rding these details, it should first be noted that neither in the present inscription nor in the signature of David Neumark given by Kaufmann, 1. c., p. 59, n. 1, after the reading by D. H. Müller, is there any mention of a rabbin
3 CONC ate held by his father. Furthermore, this frequent HaGaon must be very striking, even though people were not particularly reserved about this title in the 18th century. The line of ancestors, whose names are strangely reminiscent 
3 CONC of the Heller Levite family, does not seem to be correct either. Löwenstein gives another family tree in his “Blätter” IV, pp. 2-3. Between Naphtali Hirsch, David Neumark’s father, and Jakob Koppel, an Ahron Mirls from Vienna is i
3 CONC nserted. This would have been the progenitor of the Mirls, who owed this name to his wife Mirl, the daughter of Abraham Heller Wallerstein. For reservations about this family tree, see “Letters”, 1. c., to which it may be added th
3 CONC at at least no Ahron Fränkl has been attested in Viennese documents for the end of the 15th and beginning of the 16th century (he could only have lived then). The family tree just mentioned contains a wealth of interesting informa
3 CONC tion and provides rich instruction through Löwenstein’s comments. Individual points will be returned to in the course of this work.
3 CONT 1 Kaufmann’s correction of the Schlager’s sclion “Schlosio” in b"t b"o (Expulsion, p. 27, n. 5), as clever as it is, is wrong, since David Nouinark was not of Lovitian origin. Wachstein, Inscription. 23"
3 CONT
3 CONT The use of HaGaon in the handwritten family tree asserts he title HaGaon for four successive generations on the line. I don't find this particularly convincing for two reasons: 1) the tree may have been created by a self-promoting
3 CONC  descendant, and 2) four Gaons in a row seems excessive and exaggerated. The failure to mention the rabbinate in Fulda doesn't seem probative either. For these reasons I differ with Wachstein on this particular question. Please se
3 CONC e the compiled chart which I believe is valid despite Wachstein's doubts: https://www.geni.com/photo/view?photo_id=6000000211854581830 
