1 NAME David Joachim /Friedländer/
2 GIVN David Joachim
2 SURN Friedländer
2 _MARNM Friedländer
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Berlin-Mitte
3 STAE Berlin
3 CTRY Germany
1 BIRT
2 DATE 16 DEC 1750
2 ADDR
3 CITY Königsberg
3 STAE Hessen
3 CTRY Germany
1 DEAT
2 DATE 25 DEC 1834
2 ADDR
3 STAE Berlin
3 CTRY Germany
1 OCCU banker, writer and communal leader, Author
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} né le 16 décembre 1750 selon Samuel Sabel
2 CONT
2 CONT he was a major force in the movement for Jewish religious reform
2 CONT
2 CONT he employed Moses Mendelssohn in his silk factory, and founded the bank of "Mendelssohn and Friedländer" with Moses's son, Joseph
2 CONT
2 CONT http://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Friedl%C3%A4nder
2 CONT
2 CONT http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Friedl%C3%A4nder
2 CONT
2 CONT http://fr.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Friedl%C3%A4nder
2 CONT
2 CONT http://he.wikipedia.org/wiki/%D7%93%D7%95%D7%93_%D7%A4%D7%A8%D7%99%D7%93%D7%9C%D7%A0%D7%93%D7%A8
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.friedlaender.de/en/friedlaender.html
2 CONT
2 CONT Quelle: Judenbürgerbücher der Stadt Berlin: Nr. 7* (1792) Besitzer einer Seidenfabrik, Assessor des Manufaktur-Kollegiums, Hauptvorkämpfer der Judenemanzipation in Preußen, erster jüdischer Stadtrat in Berlin, Numismatiker.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT He was a German Jewish banker, writer and communal leader.
2 CONT
2 CONT Friedländer settled in Berlin in 1771. As the son-in-law of the rich banker Daniel Itzig, and a friend, pupil, and subsequently intellectual successor of Moses Mendelssohn, he occupied a prominent position in both Jewish and non-J
2 CONC ewish circles of Berlin. His endeavors on behalf of the Jews and Judaism included the emancipation of the Jews of Berlin and the various reforms connected therewith. Frederick William II, on his accession, called a committee whose
2 CONC  duty was to acquaint him with the grievances of the Jews, Friedländer and Itzig being chosen as general delegates. But the results of the conference were such that the Jews declared themselves unable to accept the reforms propose
2 CONC d, and not until after the French Revolution, with the edict of March 11, 1812, did the Jews then living on Prussian territory succeed in obtaining equal rights from Frederick William III.
2 CONT
2 CONT Friedländer and his friends in the community of Berlin now turned their attention to the reform of worship in harmony with modern ideas and the changed social position of the Jews. The proposition in itself was unjustified, and th
2 CONC e propositions of Friedländer, who had meanwhile been called (1813) to the conferences on the reorganization of the Jewish cult held in the Israelite Consistory of Westphalia (Royal Westphalian Consistory of the Israelites (he)) a
2 CONC t Cassel, were unacceptable to even the most radical members, as they tended to reduce Judaism to a mere colorless code of ethics.
2 CONT
2 CONT Friedländer was more successful in his educational endeavors. He was one of the founders of a Jewish free school (1778), which he directed in association with his brother-in-law, Isaac Daniel Itzig. In this school, however, exclus
2 CONC ively Jewish subjects were soon crowded out. Friedländer also wrote text-books, and was one of the first to translate the Hebrew prayer-book into German.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/David_Friedländer
2 CONT --------------------
