1 NAME Michael /Friedländer/
2 GIVN Michael
2 SURN Friedländer
2 _MARNM Friedländer
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1769
2 ADDR
3 CITY Königsberg
3 STAE East Prussia
1 DEAT
2 DATE 4 APR 1824
2 ADDR
3 CITY Paris
3 STAE Île-de-France
3 CTRY France
1 TITL Dr. med.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Friedländer, Michael (1769-1824)[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT 1782 (Oct 15): Matriculated at the university (Königsberg).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1791 (Mar 17): Doctorate of medicine (Halle).
2 CONT
2 CONT 1794: Begins a medical practice in Berlin.
2 CONT
2 CONT 1800: Moves to Paris.
2 CONT
2 CONT Michael Friedländer was born in 1769 in Königsberg as the youngest son of the merchant Meier Friedländer (1745-1808), and older brother to David Friedländer. He died on 4 April 1824 in Paris, where he had been living since 1800. H
2 CONC e was a gifted and philanthropically-minded physician, who published in both French and German medical journals.
2 CONT
2 CONT Friedländer received a liberal education at home under the direction of Isaak Euchel [bio], a former student of Kant’s, and then matriculated at the university in Königsberg on 15 October 1782, becoming a favorite student of Kant’
2 CONC s, as well as attending the lectures of C. J. Kraus [bio], Johann Schultz [bio], and Karl Hagen [bio]. In 1787 he left Königsberg to study at Berlin (under Herz and Bloch), Göttingen, and finally Halle, where he graduated in 1791 
2 CONC with a degree in medicine. He made a three year study tour through Germany, Holland, England, Scotland (remaining in Edinburgh for several months), Italy, and Switzerland, finally settling in Berlin in 1794, where he practiced med
2 CONC icine and introduced the new small pox immunization. During this time he often traveled between Berlin and Königsberg, and was likely the conduit for both news and student notes (of Kant’s lectures) from Königsberg to his uncle Da
2 CONC vid Friedländer [bio] in Berlin, who belonged to Moses Mendelssohn’s circle.
2 CONT
2 CONT Political changes motivated his departure for Paris in 1800, and he soon was facilitating the exchange of ideas between the French and German medical communities, publishing in both languages, including in Hufeland’s Journal der p
2 CONC raktische Heilkunde, Gilbert’s Annalen der Physik, Guizot’s Journal d’education, the Dictionnaire des science médicale, the Biographie universelle, and the Revue encyclopedique. He also maintained a medical practice, serving as Ma
2 CONC dame de Staël’s physician during her last years.
2 CONT
2 CONT Because he held a doctorate, he is often referred to as "D. Friedländer," for which reason some have confused references to him with his better known uncle (David). [Sources: HM, 22:232-33; Neuer Nekrolog 1824, 2:749-55; Jüdische 
2 CONC Biographisches Archiv, 255:122-30; Stark 1993, 235]
2 CONT
2 CONT Select Publications:
2 CONT
2 CONT Dissertatio inauguralis medica de calore corporis humani aucto ejusque medela (Halle: Typis Grunertianis, 1791).
2 CONT
2 CONT Entwurf einer Geschichte der Armen und Armenanstalten nebst einer Nachricht von dem jetzigen Zustande der Pariser Armenanstalten und Hospitäler insbesondere im November 1803 (Leipzig: Göschen, 1804).
2 CONT
2 CONT De l’éducation physique de l’homme (Paris: Treuttel et Würtz, 1815). Transl. into German by F. E. Oehler (Leipzig, 1819).
2 CONT
2 CONT Versuch über die innern Sinne und ihre Anomalien, Starrsucht, Entzückung, Schlafsucht und Intelligenzzerrüttung (Leipzig: Baumgärtner, 1826).
2 CONT
2 CONT [1] Friedlaender [1913] gives 1767 as the birth-year. Spanier Stammbaum-Descendants of Moses Spanier - Page 67
