1 NAME Wolf Lippmann (Abraham Benjamin) /Hamburger/
2 GIVN Wolf Lippmann (Abraham Benjamin)
2 SURN Hamburger
2 _MARNM Hamburger
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Alter Jüdischer Friedhof
2 ADDR
3 CITY Fürth
3 STAE Bavaria
3 CTRY Germany
1 BIRT
2 DATE 26 JAN 1770
2 ADDR
3 CITY Fürth
3 STAE Bavaria
3 CTRY Germany
1 DEAT
2 DATE 15 MAY 1850
2 ADDR
3 CITY Fürth
3 STAE Bavaria
3 CTRY Germany
1 OCCU Lehrer, Leiter der talmudhochschule in Fürth
1 TITL Rabbi
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} He was the "son-in-law of Zerkowitz" who, per the memoirs of Moses Porges, was living in Fuerth in 1799.
2 CONT
2 CONT cf.: http://www.fuerthwiki.de/wiki/index.php?title=Wolf_Hamburger
2 CONT
2 CONT Fuerth “wiki” biography [early life]: “His father died shortly after his birth and the mother later moved to Amsterdam to live with her second husband Benjamin Cohen Amersfort, leaving the son in difficult circumstances with guard
2 CONC ians. He studied in Fürth with Wolf Ullmann and at the yeshiva of Chief Rabbi Meshullam Salman Kohn. On September 1, 1789, Wolf Hamburger married Rachel Miriam Cohen (born in Prague, died November 26, 1836 in Fürth), a daughter of
2 CONC  Salomon Cohen [*aka Salomon Kohn-Zerkowitz] and granddaughter of the Prague magnate Samuel Lucka (1720-1792) and his wife Reichel, by whom Hamburger was supported. He and his wife had three children (two sons and a daughter), but
2 CONC  [the sons] died very early. In 1799, for health reasons, Chief Rabbi Kohn placed him in charge of the Fürth Yeshiva, which was the largest rabbinic school in western Germany during his thirty-year term. His strictness with the la
2 CONC w caused concern in the community, which probably exceeded that of his mentor Kohn. He vowed to renounce all rabbinical offices and was content with the position of collegiate rabbi at the prayer midrash of his ancestor Bermann Fr
2 CONC änkel - the ‘St. Claus Synagogue’. He allegedly rejected the chief rabbinates of Hamburg and Mainz that were offered to him.” 
2 CONT
2 CONT From The Jewish Encylopedia: “He was a contemporary of R. Moses Sofer, and is mentioned by the latter in his "Ḥatam Sofer." He wrote: (1) ‘Sha'ar ha-Zeḳenim,’ in two parts, the first containing homilies, responsa, and ethics; the 
2 CONC second, responsa on civil law (Sulzbach, 1830); (2) ‘Simlat Binyamin,’ in three parts: (a) ‘Simlat Binyamin’" responsa on the ritual laws of Oraḥ Ḥayyim and Yoreh De'ah; (b) ‘Naḥalat Binyamin,’ responsa on the ritual laws of Eben 
2 CONC ha-'Ezer, Ḥoshen Mishpaṭ, and Haggadot, followed by a treatise on circumcision and by some homilies; (c) ‘Sha'ar Binyamin,’ halakic novellæ on different sections of the Talmud (Fürth, 1840-41); (3) ‘Ḳol Bokim,’ a funeral oration o
2 CONC n the death of Meshullam Zalman Cohen (ib.1820); (4) ‘Allon Bakut,’ funeral orations on the death of Herz Scheuer and others (2 vols., ib. 1823); (5) a funeral oration on the death of Maximilian Joseph I., King of Bavaria (ib. 182
2 CONC 5). Hamburger was one of the last, if not the last, head of a yeshibah in Germany who, without holding an official position in the congregation, devoted his time to the teaching of the Talmud; his wife carried on a business, and t
2 CONC hus supported the household. Hamburger was strictly Orthodox, although opposed to religious ecstasy and mysticism ("Allg. Zeit. des Jud." 1846, pp. 266, 343). With the beginning of the Reform movement, about 1830, when the governm
2 CONC ent aided the advocates of innovations in the Jewish fold, he had to contend with many adversities of which he bitterly complains in his books (see especially preface to "Simlat Binyamin "). His yeshibah was closed, and he was for
2 CONC ced to leave the city. A great many prominent rabbis were his disciples, among them: Seligman Baer Bamberger of Würzburg, Isaac Löwy of Fürth, and David Einhorn.” 
