1 NOTE {geni:about_me} http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Leopold_Ullstein
2 CONT
2 CONT Leopold Ullstein (6 September 1826 – 4 December 1899) was the founder and publisher of several successful German newspapers, including B.Z. am Mittag and Berliner Morgenpost. Many of these are still published today.[1] Ullstein wa
2 CONC s also the founder of the leading German publishing house Ullstein-Verlag.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years[edit] Leopold Ullstein was born 6 September 1826 in Fürth, Bavaria of ethnic Jewish parents.
2 CONT
2 CONT Publishing empire[edit] Death and legacy[edit] Leopold Ullstein died 4 December 1899. He was 73 years old at the time of his death.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ullstein's publishing empire was carried on after his death by his five children, one of whom, Hermann Ullstein, emigrated from Nazi Germany in December 1938 and published a history of the Ullstein firm.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT Following the rise of Adolf Hitler and the Nazi Party in 1933, the Ullstein publishing empire was forcibly "Aryanized." In 1934 the firm, valued at 60 million marks, was sold under duress for 6 million marks.[2]
