1 NAME עמנואל /לסקר/
2 GIVN עמנואל
2 SURN לסקר
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 24 DEC 1868
2 PLAC Berlinchen (Barlinek)
2 ADDR
3 CITY Berlinchen
3 STAE Westpommern
3 CTRY Prussia
1 BURI
2 DATE JAN 1941
2 PLAC Beth Olom Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY New York
3 STAE New York
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 11 JAN 1941
2 PLAC Mount Sinai Hospital
2 ADDR
3 CITY New York
3 STAE New York
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Originally named Immanuel (birth record Berlinchen 1868 No. 158)
2 CONT
2 CONT https://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emanuel_Lasker
2 CONT
2 CONT ==Wikipedia Biographical Summary:==
2 CONT
2 CONT "...'''Emanuel Lasker''' (December 24, 1868 – January 11, 1941) was a German chess player, mathematician, and philosopher who was World Chess Champion for 27 years. In his prime Lasker was one of the most dominant champions, and h
2 CONC e is still generally regarded as one of the strongest players ever..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Emanuel Lasker was born on December 24, 1868 at Berlinchen in Neumark (now Barlinek in Poland), the son of a Jewish cantor. At the age of eleven he was sent to Berlin to study mathematics, where he lived with his brother Berth
2 CONC old, eight years his senior, who taught him how to play chess..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...At New York 1893, he won all thirteen games, one of the few times in chess history that a player has achieved a perfect score in a significant tournament..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...In spring 1933 Adolf Hitler started a campaign of discrimination and intimidation against Jews, depriving them of their property and citizenship. Lasker and his wife Martha, who were both Jewish, were forced to leave Germany i
2 CONC n the same year. After a short stay in England, in 1935 they were invited to live in the USSR by Nikolai Krylenko, the Commissar of Justice who was responsible for the Moscow show trials and, in his other capacity as Sports Minist
2 CONC er, was an enthusiastic supporter of chess. In the USSR, Lasker renounced his German citizenship and received Soviet citizenship. He took permanent residence in Moscow, and was given a post at Moscow's Institute for Mathematics...
2 CONC "
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Joseph Stalin's Great Purge started at about the same time the Laskers arrived in the USSR. In August 1937, Martha and Emanuel Lasker decided to leave the Soviet Union, and they moved, via the Netherlands, to the United States
2 CONC  (first Chicago, next New York) in October 1937..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...He died of a kidney infection in New York on January 11, 1941, at the age of 72, as a charity patient at the Mount Sinai Hospital. He was buried in the Beth Olom Cemetery, Queens, New York. He was survived by his wife Martha a
2 CONC nd his sister, Mrs. Lotta Hirschberg..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Lasker was a good friend of [http://www.geni.com/people/Albert-Einstein-%D7%90%D7%9C%D7%91%D7%A8%D7%98-%D7%90%D7%99%D7%A0%D7%A9%D7%98%D7%99%D7%99%D7%9F/4481345450320047133 Albert Einstein], who wrote the introduction to the po
2 CONC sthumous biography Emanuel Lasker, ''The Life of a Chess Master'' from Dr. Jacques Hannak (1952)..."
2 CONT
2 CONT "...Poetess Else Lasker-Schüler was his sister-in-law..."
2 CONT
2 CONT >''SOURCE'': Wikipedia contributors, 'Emanuel Lasker', ''Wikipedia, The Free Encyclopedia'', 23 September 2011, 16:12 UTC, <http://en.wikipedia.org/w/index.php?title=Emanuel_Lasker&oldid=452045036> [accessed 24 September 2011] 
